In article <HAIRSTON.90Jun11144702@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>a knowledgable player would _intentionally_ avoid pitfalls or cause
>certain things to happen. an inexperienced player would make mistakes
>and using common sense should potentially be able to undo the wrong that
>was done.

Likewise,  a knowledgeable player would set the options to avoid performing
foolish actions.  An inexperienced player wouldn't set the options.  (An
inexperienced player might get the option settings from someone else,  but
then by the same token that inexperienced player can ask the more
experienced player What Should Not Be Done.)

>i make no allowances for "line noise" or "slow response"

Why are you so elitist?  Why not make allowances for those of us who have
to play on such systems?  Does it detract from your enjoyment of the
game?

>since i wouldn't use a word processor (for example)
>under those conditions unless i had to and then i wouldn't complain.

You wouldn't complain if you had to use a word processor with response
problems?  

It's a GAME,  Dave.  People play it for FUN  (remember that word),  and
they can enjoy playing it despite occasional lags in response.  Is it
really sinful for me to play on such a system if it is my only recourse?
Is it really so horrible for me to want the game to have a design feature
that enhances my playing of the game and does not detract from other
people's enjoyment?  Shouldn't nethack be designed with enjoyable play
in mind?  (For the vast majority of cases,  the dev team has done an
excellent job of making nethack extremely enjoyable.)

The risk of death in battle (or other weird circumstances) adds to the
fun of the game.  However,  dying because someone started compiling on
my system and so I hit a movement key once too often does not add to
the fun of game.

>to me, the need/usage of confirmations is an admission of guilt in
>the sense that the interface is somehow wrong.

To me,  the need/usage of confirmations is an admission that the
hardware to interface with is limited.  After all,  do you really attack
someone by moving into them?  Of course not.  No real person would ever
get those two confused,  and if we all played in body suits with stereo
3-D panoramic displays,  we never would.  As is,  we're stuck with a
keyboard/mouse combo.  Having to specify that you wish to attack,  as
opposed to move,  in a direction would be a waste;  you attack too often
to make that a worthwhile interface characteristic.

>cruising aimlessly around a treacherous dungeon and falling into a
>potentially drowning pool of water should be a problem!

Well then,  let's make the pools invisible!

It's a GAME,  Dave,  not a matter of life and death to the players.  We're
playing to have fun.  Random death due to occasional glitches in response
time is not FUN.

>On the other hand, falling into a pool shouldn't be
>certain death either.

If we are to be realistic,  why not?  Nethack characters are almost always
loaded to the gills;  they'll sink like a rock.  Maybe you would have time
to put on a ring of levitation or teleportation,  but take off armor?  Not
a chance.

If we are to make this a FUN game,  however,  it might enhance the game
to have pools not be instant death traps.

